.TH snip-config 1  "snip 0.6.0" "Snip Manual"
.SH "NAME"
snip\-config \- inspect and modify snip configuration
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
\fBsnip config\fR [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fIsubcommands\fR>
\fBsnip config path\fR [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] 
\fBsnip config show\fR [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] 
\fBsnip config init\fR [\fB\-\-force\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] 
\fBsnip config set\fR [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fIKEY\fR> <\fIVALUE\fR> 
\fBsnip config unset\fR [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fIKEY\fR> 
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
Inspect and modify the user configuration. path prints the resolved config file, show prints effective stored values, init creates a file, set updates one supported key, and unset removes a key so the built\-in default applies again.
.PP
Unknown TOML fields are preserved when snip writes the file. Configuration key meanings and defaults are documented in snip\-config(5).
.PP
Global options are described in snip(1).
.SH "COMMANDS"
.SS "snip config"
.PP
Inspect and modify ~/.config/snip/config.toml
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.SS "snip config path"
.PP
Print the resolved config file path
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.SS "snip config show"
.PP
Print the current config (defaults to an empty schema when absent)
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.SS "snip config init"
.PP
Create a config file, optionally binding a default library
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
Replace an existing config file
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.SS "snip config set"
.PP
Set one supported config key
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
<\fIKEY\fR>
Configuration key to update
